rtes-03-meditations_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"2820","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"613","display_name":"D.E. Wittkower"}]},{"id":"146140","section_number":"7","title":"Meditation IV:  Of Truth and Error","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_descartes_dew_librivox\/descartes-04-meditations_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"1426","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"613","display_name":"D.E. Wittkower"}]},{"id":"146141","section_number":"8","title":"Meditation V:  Of the Essence of Material Things; And, Again, Of God; That He Exists","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_descartes_dew_librivox\/descartes-05-meditations_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"1121","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"613","display_name":"D.E. Wittkower"}]},{"id":"146142","section_number":"9","title":"Meditation VI:  Of the Existence of Material Things, And of the Real Distinction Between the Mind and Body of Man","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_descartes_dew_librivox\/descartes-06-meditations_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"2525","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"613","display_name":"D.E. Wittkower"}]}],"genres":[{"id":"36","name":"*Non-fiction"},{"id":"98","name":"Philosophy"}],"translators":[{"id":"6561","first_name":" John","last_name":"Veitch","dob":"1829","dod":"1894"}]},{"id":"5236","title":"Meditations from the Pen of Mrs. Maria W. Stewart","description":"Maria W. Stewart was America's first black woman political writer.  Between 1831 and 1833, she gave four speeches on the topics of slavery and women's rights.  <em>Meditations From The Pen of Mrs. Maria W. Stewart<\/em>\u2014published in 1879, shortly before her death\u2014is a collection of those speeches as well as her memoir, some meditations and prayers.  They are political, poetical and sermon all at the same time; but in the milieu in which she lectured, they were a critically important part of the abolitionist movement years before the contributions of others such as Frederick Douglass and Sojourner Truth.  Her speeches and essays espoused a return to Christian values and morality, but also proposed fundamental changes in gender roles in the midst of tremendous public opposition to the rights of blacks and of women. (Introduction by James K. White)","url_text_source":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/details\/meditationsfromp00stew","language":"English","copyright_year":"1879","num_sections":"15","url_rss":"https:\/\/librivox.org\/rss\/5236","url_zip_file":"https:\/\/archive.org\/compress\/meditations_pen_librivox\/formats=64KBPS MP3&file=\/meditations_pen_librivox.zip","url_project":"","url_librivox":"https:\/\/librivox.org\/meditations-from-the-pen-by-maria-w-stewart\/","url_other":"","totaltime":"3:55:03","totaltimesecs":14103,"authors":[{"id":"1853","first_name":"Maria W.","last_name":"Stewart","dob":"1803","dod":"1880"}],"url_iarchive":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/details\/meditations_pen_librivox","sections":[{"id":"58945","section_number":"0","title":"00 - Preface\/Letters and Commendations","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_00_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"1552","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58946","section_number":"1","title":"01 - Sufferings During The War","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_01_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"1719","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58947","section_number":"2","title":"02 - Religion - Introduction","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_02_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"694","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58948","section_number":"3","title":"03 - Religion - Prayer","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_03_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"1394","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58949","section_number":"4","title":"04 - Meditations Introduction, I, II and Prayer","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_04_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"588","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58950","section_number":"5","title":"05 - Meditations III, IV and Prayer","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_05_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"437","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58951","section_number":"6","title":"06 - Meditations V, VI, VII and Prayer","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_06_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"601","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58952","section_number":"7","title":"07 - Meditation VIII and Prayer","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_07_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"322","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58953","section_number":"8","title":"08 - Meditations IX, X and Prayer","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_08_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"731","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58954","section_number":"9","title":"09 - Meditations XI, XII and Prayer","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_09_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"572","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58955","section_number":"10","title":"10 - Meditation XIII, Prayer and Meditation XIV","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_10_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"704","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58956","section_number":"11","title":"11 - Lecture","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_11_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"862","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58957","section_number":"12","title":"12 - An Address Delivered Before The Afric-American Female Intelligence Society of Boston","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_12_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"906","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58958","section_number":"13","title":"13 - An Address Delivered at the African Masonic Hall, Boston","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_13_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"1414","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]},{"id":"58959","section_number":"14","title":"14 - Mrs. Stewart's Farewell Address To Her Friends In The City Of Boston","listen_url":"https:\/\/www.archive.org\/download\/meditations_pen_librivox\/meditations_14_stewart_64kb.mp3","language":"English","playtime":"1607","file_name":null,"readers":[{"reader_id":"5985","display_name":"James K. White"}]}],"genres":[{"id":"25","name":"Poetry"},{"id":"36","name":"*Non-fiction"},{"id":"99","name":"Political Science"},{"id":"102","name":"Religion"}],"translators":[]},{"id":"12252","title":"Meditations of the Emperor Marcus Aurelius Antoninus","description":"The Meditations is divided into 12 books that chronicle different periods of Marcus' life. Each book is not in chronological order and it was written for no one but himself. The style of writing that permeates the text is one that is simplified, straightforward, and perhaps reflecting Marcus' Stoic perspective on the text. Depending on the English translation, Marcus' style is not viewed as anything regal or belonging to royalty, but rather a man among other men which allows the reader to relate to his wisdom. \r\nA central theme to Meditations is the importance of analyzing one's judgment of self and others and the development of a cosmic perspective. As he said \"You have the power to strip away many superfluous troubles located wholly in your judgment, and to possess a large room for yourself embracing in thought the whole cosmos, to consider everlasting time, to think of the rapid change in the parts of each thing, of how short it is from birth until dissolution, and how the void before birth and that after dissolution are equally infinite\". He advocates finding one's place in the universe and sees that everything came from nature, and so everything shall return to it in due time. Another strong theme is of maintaining focus and to be without distraction all the while maintaining strong ethical principles such as \"Being a good man\". \r\nHis Stoic ideas often involve avoiding indulgence in sensory affections, a skill which will free a man from the pains and pleasures of the material world. He claims that the only way a man can be harmed by others is to allow his reaction to overpower him. An order or logos permeates existence. Rationality and clear-mindedness allow one to live in harmony with the logos. This allows one to rise above faulty perceptions of \"good\" and \"bad\". - Summary by Wikipedia","url_text_source":"https:\/\/www.gutenberg.org\/ebooks\/55317","la